    <node id="click">
      <data key="d0">click</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-a01aa3869759c0df193b39c39a682dfb-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b29ad775cde5b9e8e27a6d6eafeda4a8-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba1791b7a2e156d3fdfe4a129ba66a73-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c03a55d3ed535c691cb634704db74a30-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce3a0782e39046b81c406c6b4e1edfff-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e183c3bffbabc03a75bb2382164f1a11-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e89442d6a67c299758956cf8985a34cb-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ee53c6412436092f3c03b65b2173ad54-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f7718246a4863503f148e838eec9d4ca-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-461bb085df60d40466f8b6722f80c025-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d2">Click is an action performed by a user, typically involving the use of a mouse or other input device to press a button or activate a link on a digital interface. This action is often referred to as "clicking" and is a fundamental interaction in computer and web navigation. It can also be described as the action of performing a click, which is an action verb used to denote this specific user interaction. The term "click" is commonly used in various contexts, including web design, software development, and user experience (UX) to describe the act of selecting an element on a screen.&lt;SEP&gt;An action performed by a user to select an item on a screen.</data>
      <data key="d1">method</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1787055926</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="screen_id">
      <data key="d0">screen_id</data>
      <data key="d3">doc-b29ad775cde5b9e8e27a6d6eafeda4a8-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b6a4ebc813bac50eb0d53c08cb4b08da-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ba90d6ca176ed689081def0260ad0880-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-bf0db5826ed0280c954ef6c7dc9c6466-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c02f14b1e32cdfab5456f0d0d6deb773-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c03a55d3ed535c691cb634704db74a30-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-c9c69e2a34c295b1586bd3f53b868b4d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cba959f3b1e47acab4a419f1afb756d1-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ce3a0782e39046b81c406c6b4e1edfff-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e0a00ecf4aa98abb75f058611cd0bfdf-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-024b19b8de0d5b640f27f0a312da5ca5-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-1abb292a251278234ef2aea5585a4cec-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-323e293277d50c431bbc02b71c78a7a1-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-45c24883b260d58ce196b2bd5813a10a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-575df4d1d4a9a71fa3b40c1f3ed4f6be-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e89442d6a67c299758956cf8985a34cb-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ee53c6412436092f3c03b65b2173ad54-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f067742834b4ccdbde208903ac4ed06c-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f437e42ae366198509c91f29b833f612-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f7718246a4863503f148e838eec9d4ca-chunk-000</data>
      <data key="d2">Screen ID is a term used across various contexts related to user interactions on a website or application. It serves as a unique identifier for different screens or locations within a digital interface. Specifically, it can refer to the screen where the account logging in process occurs, often denoted by the value 'account_logged_in'. Another common usage of screen ID involves identifying the location where a minicart open event happens, which might be represented by the value 'minicart_open'. Additionally, there is a screen ID associated with the cart containing items, labeled as 'cart_with_item'. These IDs help in tracking user navigation and specific actions within the application, ensuring accurate analytics and user experience management.&lt;SEP&gt;Screen identifier</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
      <data key="d5">1787055544</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
